Rocky Linux 10 SBC-Generic (RISC-V)

This image is intended for RISC-V SBCs, including the StarFive VisionFive2,
and can also be used in QEMU. It uses ext4 and includes cloud-utils-growpart.
The root filesystem doesn't expand automatically when you boot from a
larger disk or SD card.

Expanding the root filesystem
----------------------------

First, check which partition contains your root filesystem:

    findmnt -no SOURCE,FSTYPE /
    lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,TYPE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINTS

The commands below assume that / is ext4 on /dev/vda3 and that partition
3 is the last partition on /dev/vda. Check your output before continuing;
your device names may differ. The disk must already have unused space
after the root partition. In QEMU, enlarge the virtual disk first; on a
VisionFive2, write the image to an SD card larger than the image.

Preview the partition change without writing it:

    sudo growpart -N /dev/vda 3

Then grow the partition and the filesystem, in that order:

    sudo growpart /dev/vda 3
    sudo resize2fs /dev/vda3

growpart takes the whole disk and a separate partition number.
resize2fs takes the root partition. If your root is /dev/mmcblk0p3,
use /dev/mmcblk0 3 for growpart and /dev/mmcblk0p3 for resize2fs.
You can resize this mounted ext4 filesystem without rebooting.

If growpart reports NOCHANGE, the partition may already fill the disk.
Check lsblk again. If the partition has already grown, resize2fs can
still expand the filesystem to fill it.

Check the result:

    df -h /

The available space should now reflect the larger disk or SD card.
